germany's three candidates to replace  angela merkel as chancellor have clashed  in their second tv debate front-runner  olaf schultz of the social democrats was  hoping to extend his lead in the polls  as he faced a grilling from his  conservative rival amen lascit third  place candidate annalina balbach of the  greens tried to make up lost ground  voters in germany's go to the polls to  choose a new parliament on september  26th  let's check in on those snap polls now  that were taken just after the debate  around 20 percent uh of respondents said  here that conservative candidate amin la  should perform best in the debate  meanwhile over 30 percent said schultz  was actually the most convincing making  him the winner among the three uh the  greens candidate annalina berbock  performed only marginally better than  her conservative rival amin lashit but  that put on angela merkel's party at the  losing end of this tv debate that being  said 21 think that all of the candidates  performed equally well in this debate  and we have our political correspondence  brunch with us to put this all into  context for us you watched the debate so  who do you think came out on top here  one has to say that in the end it was  olaf schultz who who did best in this  but uh one has to add that uh what  annalina barbour the green candidate did  uh was also quite impressive she  uh is not under the same sort of  pressure as the other two because it's  clear that she's not going to be the  winner in this election she's not going  to be chancellor she has a lot more room  to maneuver and she came across a lot  fresher a lot more decisive in her  answers  so i think while olaf schultz was  certainly the convincing candidate for  the majority of the viewers um i think  annalina babbok did very well as well  what about angela merkel's party the cdu  their candidate amin lashed he really  needed to get a boost in this debate did  he get that  what he's done in recent days is he's  become a lot more aggressive and there  were quite a number of scenes in this  debate where he especially attacked the  social democrat olaf schultz um there  were some quite um  harsh  exchanges between the two of them but  for i mean luscious this comes across as  a little bit artificial he's actually a  very nice guy and uh where he started  his campaign a couple of months ago  on the basis of being a nice guy of not  provoking uh uh  any of the other candidates of simply  waiting and seeing and that has turned  out to be the wrong strategy so now he's  trying to put up this fight  and he's doing that according a kind of  a according to a kind of script but it  comes across a little bit artificial  what were the biggest issues that they  discussed in this debate it was a very  uh domestically focused debate there  were issues such as pensions healthcare  housing industrial policy that sort of  thing  for foreign viewers there wasn't really  much in it there was some debate about  germany's role in nato that has to do  with the socialist left party which  criticizes nato very strongly and might  be a possible coalition partner for the  social democrats there was also some  mention for the need for foreign workers  or foreign employees to come into  germany experts in various fields  because germany really does need them i  think all candidates agree that that is  the case you mentioned that it's  becoming pretty clear that the greens  candidate analyst that she's likely not  going to be chancellor but how what are  the chances for the greens to do well in  this election oh they're definitely  going to do well they're not going to  come out as the strongest party which  seemed possible a couple of months ago  but they're still going to probably  double the amount of votes that they had  during the last election four years ago  and that makes them a very strong  contender for any government that is  going to be formed it's very likely that  the greens will be part of the next  government so that position is a very  strong one and the fact that they are so  strong on the climate issues on the  climate policy  will mean i think that that will play a  major role in whatever government we're  going to get after after this election  the question is which government that  will be i know there are a lot of  coalition possibilities but  if you just give us an overview what  looks most likely at the moment well  this is what's very new in german  politics there's basically  three parties three major parties that  are almost  at a similar level the ones that were  participating in this debate but there  are two or three other smaller parties  that could also be part of a coalition  probably are necessary for a coalition  the free democrats the business friendly  party and the socialist left party that  i mentioned amongst them um so the  permutations are really really  complicated and  it's going to be very important who's  going to be the strongest candidate  because the person who wins whose party  wins the most votes can take the  initiative in forming a coalition so if  the social democrats do that that's  going to be a very different way of  seeing a new government than if the  conservatives were to win  our political correspondent hans brown  thanks for your analysis
 

